[h1]RimMCP[/h1]
[i]Agentic intelligence - Now brought to the Rim.[/i]

[h2]What is this?[/h2]
RimMCP connects your colony to any AI agent that speaks MCP. Your agent will be able to see and do almost anything in the game: lay out zones, build, order pawns, run time forward, and tell you anything about the game.

[i]For the modern high-impact 10x engineer looking to utilize agentic AI to drive business value in their colony.[/i]

[h2]What can it do?[/h2]
[list]
[*][b]See what you see.[/b] Pawns, stockpiles, bills, research, threats, the map itself, even your mod list and error log. Keep up with alerts, letters and what happened while you weren't looking.
[*][b]Tell you what's wrong.[/b] Why nobody is cooking, why the field isn't sown, what's about to rot.
[*][b]Run the colony.[/b] Zones, stockpiles, blueprints, mining, bills, work priorities, policies, research. Assign beds and thrones to their owners.
[*][b]Command your pawns.[/b] Draft, move, attack, rescue, tend, equip. Capture, arrest, strip, or prioritize a job through their right-click menus.
[*][b]Time Control.[/b] Let time run, and pause the moment something important happens.
[*][b]Make decisions.[/b] Answer letters, accept quests, pick rewards, and handle the questions that follow. The dialogs stay on screen, so you can answer first.
[*][b]Push the buttons.[/b] Power switches, doors held open, medical beds, hold fire, mortar targets and psycasts. Supported buttons and right-click orders from your mods work too.
[*][b]Start from scratch.[/b] Pick a scenario, storyteller and difficulty, generate a world, find a starting site, build an ideoligion, and roll colonists to your criteria. All through the game's setup screens while you watch. You approve the start unless you enable unattended setup.
[*][b]Save your place.[/b] Save, load, or head back to the main menu. Check a save's game version and mods before loading it.
[/list]

[table]
[tr][th]Access[/th][th]Agents can[/th][/tr]
[tr][td][b]Full control[/b] (default)[/td][td]Do everything, including storyteller, difficulty, saves and new games.[/td][/tr]
[tr][td][b]Control[/b][/td][td]Run the colony, including letters and quests, but not change game settings, save, load or set up games.[/td][/tr]
[tr][td][b]Administration[/b][/td][td]Read, manage colony settings and run time, but not give direct orders.[/td][/tr]
[tr][td][b]Read only[/b][/td][td]Look, but no actions.[/td][/tr]
[/table]

Fine-tune access by switching individual tools on or off. The RimMCP button on the bottom bar lets you pause agents or change their access in a click, and the Activity tab shows what they've been doing.

[h2]Things to try[/h2]
[list]
[*]"Anyone worth trying to recruit from the injured raiders?"
[*]"Make a potato field on the rich soil, queue up meals."
[*]"Figure out the most efficient fabric available, order clothes for the colony and set up apparel policies."
[*]"Which mod is throwing that red error and why?"
[*]"Destroy the Mechhive, make no mistakes."
[*]"Find a mountain base with a long growing season, roll me a doctor and a decent cook."
[*]"Catch me up on what happened and check whether any of these quest rewards are worth the trouble."
[*]"Turn off the idle benches and stop holding the freezer door open."
[/list]

[h2]Requirements[/h2]
[list]
[*][b]RimWorld 1.6 or 1.7[/b].
[*]An AI client with support for local (stdio) MCP servers. Tested with Codex, Claude Code & OpenCode.
[/list]

[h2]Setup[/h2]
[olist]
[*]Enable RimMCP in your mod list and restart the game.
[*]Go to Options > Mod settings > RimMCP and click [b]Copy MCP config[/b].
[*]Add that config to your client's MCP settings (or paste it to your agent and tell it to configure itself, who has time to edit tomls nowadays).
[*]Turn on RimWorld's [b]Run in background[/b] option so requests work while you're alt-tabbed.
[/olist]

[h2]Frequently asked[/h2]
[b]Mid-save compatible?[/b] Yes. RimMCP does not save anything on your colony. Feel free to add or remove at will.

[b]Performance?[/b] Near 0 impact when idling. When your agent calls tools, most requests are handled in milliseconds.

[b]Multiplayer Mod?[/b] Your agent can look, but anything that changes the game or the clock is refused during a Multiplayer session.

[b]Privacy?[/b] If your client uses a cloud model, it may send colony data to that provider. Your colony data could help shape the mind of future LLMs, what could possibly go wrong?

